<header><p class="enc-kicker"><a href="/inventions?focus=horse-collar">Inventions &amp; Discoveries</a> · Farming and food</p><h1 class="enc-title">Horse collar</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">late 5th century</span> · <a href="/centuries/5th-century">5th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">A horse collar is a padded harness collar that spreads a load across a horse's shoulders and chest without pressing on its windpipe; it was invented in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/china">China</a> by the early <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/middle-ages">medieval period</a>. The earliest certain depiction is a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dunhuang">Dunhuang</a> cave mural of 477–499 CE, from the Northern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cao-wei">Wei dynasty</a>. It spread westward and was clearly in use in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/europe">Europe</a> by 1000, where horses came to be used more widely for ploughing, working roughly 50 per cent faster than oxen.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/horse-collar.jpg" alt="Horse collar" width="320" height="206" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Unknown author · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Earliest_equine_harness_477_499_cave_257.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">What it is</h2><p>A horse collar is a component of horse harness that distributes the force of a load across the horse's shoulders and chest, so that the horse can pull without pressure on its windpipe. It is padded and encircles the lower neck in an oval. It supports a pair of rigid curved bars called hames, usually of wood or metal, which are the anchor points for the traces, the straps or chains running to the vehicle or load. Collars are made of leather or synthetic materials stuffed with straw, foam or horsehair.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Origins</h2><p>Early horse-drawn vehicles used throat-and-girth harnesses, in which the load was carried on a strap round the horse's barrel and a breastplate mainly kept that strap from slipping back. In ancient <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/china">China</a> the breastcollar harness improved traction by placing the load across the chest. The rigid, padded horse collar followed, invented in China by the early <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/middle-ages">medieval period</a>. The earliest legitimate depiction is on a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dunhuang">Dunhuang</a> cave mural in cave 257, of the Northern Wei dynasty, dated 477–499 CE. The collar spread westward and was clearly in use in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/europe">Europe</a> by 1000, becoming common by the 12th century.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">How it was used</h2><p>Before the collar, oxen were the main farm animals, since only they could wear the earlier harnesses without harm, and a heavy plough in difficult soil might need up to sixteen of them. Horses work roughly 50 per cent faster than oxen. Combined with the horseshoe and the heavy plough, the collar helped raise food production in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/europe">Europe</a>, and the surplus allowed labour to specialise and a merchant class to emerge.</p><p>In 1910 the French cavalry officer Lefebvre des Noëttes found that two horses in a throat-and-girth harness could pull about 1,100 lb, while one horse in a collar could draw about one and a half tons. Studies in 1977 by Spruytte and Mary Aiken Littauer, followed by Georges Raepsaet, used more accurately reconstructed ancient harnesses and suggested that they could pull nearly as much. They argued that the main advantage of the collar was the lower point of attachment, which made horses more effective for ploughing.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Horse collar” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="inv-entry"><h2 class="enc-section-title">On the inventions tree</h2><dl class="inv-entry-facts"><div><dt>First known</dt><dd>477–499 CE</dd></div><div><dt>Where</dt><dd>Dunhuang, China</dd></div></dl><p class="inv-entry-note"><span class="inv-k">Note</span>An earlier, doubtful depiction comes from a 3rd-century tomb in Sichuan, and some see a partial collar in ancient Rome.</p><div class="inv-entry-rel"><span class="inv-k">Built on</span><a class="inv-chip" href="/encyclopedia/plough">Plough<span>c. 2800 BCE</span></a><a class="inv-chip" href="/encyclopedia/domestication-of-the-horse">Domestication of the horse<span>c. 2200 BCE</span></a></div><p class="enc-article-foot"><a class="enc-random-link" href="/inventions?focus=horse-collar">See it on the inventions tree &rarr;</a></p></section>
